<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- Storage: API 31-32 still needs the broad READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE,
         API 33+ uses the granular audio-only permission (spec 3.7). -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_MEDIA_AUDIO" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PLAYBACK" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!-- required="false" is the difference between "runs on any Android
         device" and "runs on phones the manufacturer wired for USB OTG".
         This was required="true", which is a HARD install/visibility
         filter: Play hides the app outright from every device without
         USB host mode, and sideloading onto one can fail with
         INSTALL_FAILED_MISSING_SHARED_LIBRARY / a feature-check refusal
         depending on the installer. The app does not need USB host to
         function — with no DAC attached it plays through the phone's
         own output and honestly reports BIT-PERFECT: NO (spec 3.6, last
         row of the table). The USB DAC path is an enhancement, not a
         requirement, so it is declared as one. -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.usb.host"
        android:required="false" />

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:appCategory="audio"
        android:requestLegacyExternalStorage="true"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Black.NoTitleBar.Fullscreen"
        android:hardwareAccelerated="false">

        <activity
            android:name=".FlastNativeActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|smallestScreenSize|density|keyboard|keyboardHidden|navigation|uiMode|layoutDirection|fontScale|locale">

            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.lib_name"
                android:value="flacplayer_native" />

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <service
            android:name=".PlaybackService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="mediaPlayback" />

    </application>

    <!--
    Notes on the attributes above that are easy to misread as cosmetic:

    android:hardwareAccelerated="false"
        Measured, not assumed. On a Galaxy A55 cold start with the DAC
        dialog never shown, `dumpsys meminfo com.flast` still reported
        GL mtrack 1,156 KB — so HWUI was being initialised for this
        process even though NativeActivity calls Window.takeSurface() and
        this app draws every pixel itself with ANativeWindow_lock. That
        is a GPU context and its buffers, maintained for a View hierarchy
        that never draws anything.

        This was only safe to turn off once the DAC volume question moved
        out of an AlertDialog and into a native screen (see
        draw_screen_dac_question in ui_main.c). With that gone the app
        owns no Views at all, so there is nothing left that software
        rendering could degrade. Runtime permission dialogs are drawn by
        the system process, not this one, and are unaffected.

        If any device ever shows a blank or garbled surface, this single
        attribute is the first thing to flip back to "true".

    android:requestLegacyExternalStorage="true"
        Only has any effect on API 29 (Android 10), which is the single
        release where scoped storage blocks direct file-path reads of
        shared storage with no FUSE fallback. API 26-28 predate scoped
        storage; API 30+ restore path access to media files through FUSE.
        Without this the C scanner finds nothing on Android 10
        specifically — a silent, one-version-wide hole that would look
        exactly like "the app does not work on my phone".

    android:configChanges="…density|uiMode|fontScale|locale…"
        Every configuration change NOT listed here destroys and recreates
        the Activity, which for a NativeActivity means android_main() is
        torn down and restarted on a brand-new thread, reloading the
        whole music library. Rotation and screen size were already
        handled; this adds the rest of what a real device throws at an
        app (dark-mode toggle, system font-size change, folding and
        unfolding, plugging in a keyboard, changing locale).
        on_cmd()/APP_CMD_CONFIG_CHANGED re-reads the display density and
        draw_frame() re-reads the window size every frame, so the UI
        adapts to all of them without a restart.
    -->

</manifest>
